(earth; he governed them himself, and often conversed with their chiefs.) Tj T*
(Sometimes through tenderness for his people he ordered them to massacre) Tj T*
(each other; and at one time twenty three thousand were put to death by) Tj T*
(their own citizens at the express command of God.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(God ordered one of their kings to murder every man of a nation they had) Tj T*
(vanquished; the king had the audacity to spare some who were not in a) Tj T*
(state to defend themselves and was punished for it. A son of this king) Tj T*
(was condemned to die for eating honey on the day of battle, and God, who) Tj T*
(was justly irritated at the father as well as son, proscribed them both,) Tj T*
(and made choice of a new king.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(This king \(whom God had expressly chosen\) committed adultery with the) Tj T*
(wife of one of his generals, and massacred her husband. By the adultress) Tj T*
(he had a son who kept seven hundred wives and three hundred concubines) Tj T*
(in his seraglio; but you must know these two kings were cherished by our) Tj T*
(God; both had heavenly benedictions heaped on their heads. The father) Tj T*
(was the man after God's own heart, and the son was the wisest of men.) Tj T*
(The Son of God, who became a man, descended in a direct line from this) Tj T*
(_wisest of men_, and from the adultress of whom we have just spoken.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(O, gentlemen, exclaimed I, you make me shudder at your impious ideas.) Tj T*
(They resumed. Have we not told you that the conduct of this God was) Tj T*
(always mysterious, purposely to humble our weak reason? The first) Tj T*
(legislator whom God gave to his favourite people was an assassin; but) Tj T*
(he had nevertheless the gift of performing a number of miracles. He) Tj T*
(composed a body of civil and religious rites and laws which we still) Tj T*
(revere as having been inspired by the Deity. And yet, you do not observe) Tj T*
(them? No; truly. We hold those people in horror who do so. It is true,) Tj T*
(that this was formerly the favourite people of God, and all other) Tj T*
(nations were rejected; afterwards the other nations were chosen, and) Tj T*
(this favourite people rejected. Do you not admire, Sir, the wisdom of) Tj T*
(the God we adore?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(At this discourse, I stole away from them, and could scarcely persuade) Tj T*
(myself it was more than a dream. Having before seen to what great) Tj T*
(perfection this people had attained in every human science, I began to) Tj T*
(fear the weakness of my nature, and determined to return to my country;) Tj T*
(lest those abominable European prejudices should make me forget my duty) Tj T*
(to my fellow creatures, and reverence for the God of all worlds.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(THE END.) Tj T*
